  "worked_solution": "Let $S$ be the set of all sequences. The given statement can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\,$ For all $s\\in S,$ $s$ is arithmetic.\n\nIntroducing the universal quantifier, we get the following:\n\n$\\,$ $\u2200s\\in S,\\,s\\text{\u00a0is arithmetic}.$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u00ac(\u2200s\\in S,\\,s\\text{\u00a0is arithmetic}) & \u2261\u2203s\\in S,\\,\u00ac(s\\text{\u00a0is arithmetic}) \\\\  & \u2261\u2203s\\in S,\\,s\\text{\u00a0is not arithmetic}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich reads \n\n$\\,$ There exists $s\\in S$ such that $s$ is not arithmetic.\n\nor simply\n\n$\\,$ Some sequences are not arithmetic.",
